WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.400
What's worse than devoting your energy to
mastering the impossible?

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.390
Or spending countless hours of your precious
time completing an epic trilogy, only to have

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.560
the conclusion slap you in your digital face?

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:13.560
Not much.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.560
"Alright, hit the Change of Venue button!

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:15.560
Now!"

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:17.640
"Aw, no.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.300
You're in a Texas!"

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.060
"Yeee-haw!"

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:27.600
Here are some of the most insulting endings
in the history of video games.

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:30.010
Warning: massive spoilers ahead.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:31.560
Ghosts 'n Goblins

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:37.219
This 1985 side-scrolling classic is probably
the first video game with a truly insulting

00:00:37.219 --> 00:00:42.210
ending — which really stings, since the
game is pretty solid, aside from its absurd

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.260
difficulty.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:48.210
Your eyes aren't deceiving you: that knight
is fighting monsters in his undies.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:52.409
Hits make you lose your armor until you're
basically questing in the buff.

00:00:52.409 --> 00:00:57.519
Developed by Capcom for arcades but released
on several home consoles, Ghosts 'n Goblins

00:00:57.519 --> 00:01:02.699
rewards players who beat it with the following
text: "THIS ROOM IS AN ILLUSION AND A TRAP

00:01:02.699 --> 00:01:04.629
DEVISED BY SATAN.

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:06.369
GO AHEAD DAUNTLESSLY!

00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:08.009
MAKE RAPID PROGRES!"

00:01:08.009 --> 00:01:10.930
Yep, that's "progress" with one "s."

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.380
"And leave off the last S for..."

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.380
"SATAN!"

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:21.009
If seeing a misspelled message in return for
your tireless efforts isn't insulting enough,

00:01:21.009 --> 00:01:26.630
players are then tasked with playing the game
again, but this time at an even harder difficulty.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:27.630
Why?

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.219
So you can see the true ending.

00:01:30.219 --> 00:01:32.299
What is this true ending, you ask?

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:36.560
Surely it must be worth the brutal, controller-breaking
second-playthrough, right?

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.990
Well, you be the judge: "CONGRATULATION.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:45.020
THIS STORY IS HAPPY END" is the sloppily-translated
message you're greeted with.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:50.509
Followed by, "BEING THE WISE AND COURAGEOUS
KNIGHT THAT YOU ARE, YOU FEEL STRONGTH WELLING

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:51.509
IN YOUR BODY."

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:55.770
Ah, yes, that familiar feeling: Strongth.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:59.429
What is the great task now required of such
a valiant soldier?

00:01:59.429 --> 00:02:01.530
"RETURN TO STARTING POINT.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:02.740
CHALLENGE AGAIN!"

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.509
This game is a trap devised by Satan.

00:02:05.509 --> 00:02:07.170
Fable 2

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:12.310
This 2008 open-world RPG sequel is considered
by many to have one of the worst endings to

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.360
any video game in history.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:15.530
Where to even start?

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:21.450
First, in a lengthy, epic role playing game,
one would at least expect a halfway decent

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.349
final confrontation, right?

00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:28.980
Sure, not every video game can have the best
ending ever, but gamers like a little challenge!

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:35.190
So what challenge does Lionhead Studios present
players of Fable 2, in the game's epic conclusion?

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.430
Press a button.

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:37.430
Any button.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.099
Just press a button and the villain dies.

00:02:39.099 --> 00:02:42.470
In fact, even if you don't press a button,
he dies.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:46.430
You literally don't have to do anything to
defeat the final boss.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.430
Epic!

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:53.180
To add insult to injury, Fable 2, a game all
about choices, gives you a measly three final

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.060
game-ending choices, all of which are relatively
inconsequential and none of which make much

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.060
sense.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:04.410
In virtually every way, Fable 2 sets the standard
for how not to end an otherwise-fantastic

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:05.410
RPG.

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:09.209
Of course, that was the standard...until…

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:11.170
Mass Effect 3

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:15.079
Not everyone was insulted by BioWare's ending
to its epic trilogy.

00:03:15.079 --> 00:03:17.209
But those people weren't paying attention.

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:22.700
The result of dozens — if not hundreds — of
hours of exploring, fighting, relationship

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:27.740
building, planet mining, decision making and
cringey romance, the ending to 2012's Mass

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:35.920
Effect 3 presents players with essentially
three decisions: control, destroy, or synthesize.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.900
All things considered, it's a bit like choosing
your favorite color: red, green, or blue.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.010
In other words: boring.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.010
"What!

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.640
Is your favorite color?"

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:46.640
"Blue!"

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:47.640
"Right.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:48.640
Off you go!"

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:49.860
"Oh, thank you.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.969
Thank you very much."

00:03:51.969 --> 00:03:55.790
That's more or less the only thing that changes
in each ending sequence.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:04:00.310
Not even the most hardcore of Mass Effect
fans could justify this ending as being anything

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:01.420
less than offensive.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:07.239
In a game that's all about choice, agency,
and the practical effects of your choices,

00:04:07.239 --> 00:04:11.230
every single player experienced almost the
exact same ending.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:16.180
The reaction was so negative, BioWare even
released a rewritten and free DLC ending to

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:18.030
try to make up for it.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.550
Borderlands

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:23.400
Gearbox Software's role-playing shooter is
no stranger to silliness.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:29.110
However, the game's over-the-top nature simply
can't justify its obnoxiously offensive ending.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:34.420
After 25 to 30 hours of frantic first-person
shooting and role-playing — or 60 hours,

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:38.950
if you're a completionist — Borderlands
rewards players' quest for the Vault with

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:41.040
a not-so-pleasant surprise.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:46.100
As we already learned during gameplay, the
Vault can only be opened every 200 years — and

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:50.880
by the time we've finished dispatching the
Destroyer, a monstrous guardian of the prized

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.030
location, the Vault gets sealed for another
200 years.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:58.530
Needless to say, this ending didn't sit too
well with fans.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:02.940
Nothing like playing through an entire game
searching for pieces of a key to open a legendary

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:07.480
vault, only to have said vault shut in your
face for another three lifetimes.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:11.330
At least Claptrap is turned into an interplanetary
ninja assassin.

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:15.720
But that's still just the insulting icing
on the disappointment cake.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:17.350
No Man's Sky

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:22.820
This 2016 open galaxy indie sensation has
proven to be one of the most disappointing

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:25.730
games in recent memory — if not of all time.

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:30.250
However, the game might have had a shred of
a redeeming quality if, when players arrived

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:35.060
at the Center of the Galaxy, there was a surprise
there that made the entire, lonely, monotonous

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:36.500
journey worth it.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:40.350
Not only is there no surprise at the Center
of the Universe — there's literally nothing

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.220
at the Center of the Universe.

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:43.450
Nothing!

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:44.740
"What's in the box?!"

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:48.980
"Nothing!

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:52.080
Absolutely nothing!"

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.840
To those gamers lucky enough to have skipped
this ultimately unremarkable game, it's like

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:57.840
this.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.320
Just imagine putting yourself through hours
upon hours of almost mindless drudgery, until

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.331
the fateful moment arrives when you finally
can click on the Center of the Universe, with

00:06:06.331 --> 00:06:12.340
the teeny, tiny, infinitesimal hopes that
somehow, some way, it might all be worth it.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:17.550
Then you just watch the map zoom by for several
minutes before beginning … a new game?

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:18.940
Not quite.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:23.320
You keep all of your stuff, but your ship
is broken and you're in a new universe continuing

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:26.330
your journey with all knowledge and experience
kept.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:29.410
It just breaks your stuff and spits you out!

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:30.410
That's it.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:34.500
What could possibly be a worse ending to such
an incredibly disappointing game than asking

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:35.980
players to do it all again?

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:40.740
You've gotta give Hello Games credit for one
thing — it successfully managed to insult

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:44.690
just about everyone who played this massively
overhyped project.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:45.690
"Stupid!

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.650
You're so stupid!"

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:50.030
Halo 2

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:55.530
Who can forget popping this 2004 Xbox classic
into that hulking black beast of a video game

00:06:55.530 --> 00:07:00.020
console and drifting away into beautiful,
first-person, space-shooting bliss?

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:05.150
Yes, those were the good times … until the
abrupt and totally insulting cliffhanger posing

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:09.290
as a legitimate ending made us all stare at
the screen, dumbfounded, and ask:

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:11.220
"Is that it?"

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:14.930
Although it was apparently not the ending
the creators wanted to make, players were

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:19.430
left with an incredibly unsatisfying taste
in their mouths when legendary spaceman Master

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:23.560
Chief, secretly aboard the spaceship called
The Truth, says this:

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:26.850
"Do you mind telling me what you're doing
on that ship?"

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.820
"Sir, finishing this fight."

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.440
But then he doesn't finish anything.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:32.870
The game cuts to black.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:34.160
The end music plays.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:35.160
Roll credits.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:36.780
Have fun waiting for the sequel!

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:38.940
Should be two or three years, tops.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:43.460
Anyone who looks back fondly at this cliffhanger
as an epic moment in gaming history is either

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.690
living in denial or a victim of revisionist
history.

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:49.550
The game may have been awesome, but the ending
was awful.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:51.950
Batman: Arkham Asylum

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:55.480
After the amazing gameplay experience of the
first installment in the Arkham series of

00:07:55.480 --> 00:08:01.270
Batman games, players couldn't wait for the
epic finale Rocksteady Studios had concocted.

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:05.500
Unfortunately, the ending to this 2009 title
is weak, to put it mildly.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:10.370
The Joker himself is anything but weak, as
he injects himself with Titan, a super-steroid

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:12.740
that makes the someone massively muscular.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.050
And then we get a Super-strong Joker!

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:19.270
Because that's what fans have always been
clamoring for: the Joker plus muscles.

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:19.760
Right?

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:20.260
"Yeah."

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:22.260
Yeah — no.

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:24.990
The ending that comes from this fight is a
serious letdown.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:30.080
A steroid-inflated Joker reeks of a lack of
creativity, and the whole game culminates

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:31.990
in a forgettable boss battle.

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:36.620
For a villain who's always played mind games
with the caped crusader, using brute strength

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:38.279
simply doesn't sit well.

00:08:38.279 --> 00:08:42.009
The game fails at even doing anything remotely
interesting with the of an idea.

00:08:42.009 --> 00:08:43.710
It's just a standard boss fight.

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:44.750
And then it's over.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:49.430
Although the game itself is objectively very,
very good, even the most optimistic of Batman

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:52.899
fans can't help but admit that the ending
is pretty lame.

00:08:52.899 --> 00:08:54.980
Final Fantasy X

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:56.149
Unsatisfied.

00:08:56.149 --> 00:08:57.319
Confused.

00:08:57.319 --> 00:08:58.490
Bored.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:08:59.660
Underwhelmed.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:04.269
These are not feelings you want to experience
after devoting between 80 and 200 hours to

00:09:04.269 --> 00:09:05.269
a video game.

00:09:05.269 --> 00:09:09.610
Yet, that's what many players were left feeling
after watching what passes for an ending in

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:11.420
2001's Final Fantasy X.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:16.730
With the villainous Sin defeated, Tidus and
Yuna can finally let the good times roll,

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:18.910
get married, and have some babies, right?

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:21.699
It's party time for the summoner and crew,
surely.

00:09:21.699 --> 00:09:23.639
But wait … Tidus is a ghost?

00:09:23.639 --> 00:09:24.939
Or a dream?

00:09:24.939 --> 00:09:26.250
Tidus isn't real?

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:28.089
What exactly is happening?

00:09:28.089 --> 00:09:33.040
Even by Final Fantasy standards, this ending
is more than a bit convoluted, and a whole

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:38.750
lot of lame — so lame, in fact, that a sequel
was required to even get close to justifying

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:42.290
this insulting, anticlimactic trash ending.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:45.519
Star Wars Knights of the Old Republic II:
The Sith Lords

00:09:45.519 --> 00:09:50.310
Nothing reeks of "rushed ending" quite like
a few minutes of dreadfully boring dialogue

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:53.060
you desperately wish you could fast-forward
through.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:57.089
All with a somewhat-secondary character about
hypothetical scenarios.

00:09:57.089 --> 00:10:01.300
Which fail to actually detail the fates of
characters you've already invested dozens

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:02.620
of hours into.

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:07.649
Sadly, this is what players of this otherwise
excellent 2004 sequel are left with.

00:10:07.649 --> 00:10:12.509
Knights of the Old Republic II is an all-round
terrific Star Wars role-playing game, blending

00:10:12.509 --> 00:10:16.630
a great storyline with solid combat into an
engaging experience.

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:19.310
The ending, however, is anything but engaging.

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:21.350
It's boring and loaded with baloney.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:24.770
Though, when you think about it, it is a prequel
series.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:28.629
And we all know how those Star Wars prequels
tend to go...

00:10:28.629 --> 00:10:29.829
Rampage

00:10:29.829 --> 00:10:34.990
It may not have worst ending of its generation
— because "strongth" — but the ending

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:39.209
to this 1986 arcade classic still hits you
right where it hurts.

00:10:39.209 --> 00:10:42.440
Maybe your sad childhood memory here goes
something like this.

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:47.060
You stayed up all night with your best friend,
determined to beat the NES port of Rampage.

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:49.139
Perhaps it all started like this:

00:10:49.139 --> 00:10:50.139
"Whoa!

00:10:50.139 --> 00:10:51.139
Nice graphics!

00:10:51.139 --> 00:10:52.139
I'd like to get my hands on that game!"

00:10:52.139 --> 00:10:53.980
"You mean you haven't played it yet?"

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:59.080
For a game called Rampage starring giant monsters,
it's actually kinda dull, even when competing

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:03.509
cooperatively with a friend, but you wouldn't
turn it off until you discovered what was

00:11:03.509 --> 00:11:05.899
at the end of this nationwide disaster.

00:11:05.899 --> 00:11:08.720
The ending would justify the experience, right?

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:09.880
It has to.

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:15.370
Finally, you get back to California, level
every building, dodge all sorts of military-grade

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:19.660
projectiles, and wait anxiously for your reward,
and then ...

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:20.740
"CONGRATULATIONS!"

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:21.960
That's it?

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:32.139
Thank you, Rampage, for ruining at least five
hours of America's childhood.

00:11:32.139 --> 00:11:33.139
"Stupid!

00:11:33.139 --> 00:11:35.870
You're so stupid!"

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:37.769
Thanks for watching!

00:11:37.769 --> 00:11:40.329
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:11:40.329 --> 00:11:42.309
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

